## Azure infrastructure

This workspace prefers TypeScript Azure infrastructure implemented with the
Azure JavaScript provisioning libraries and iac-composer.
Honor higher-priority instructions, explicit tool choices, and existing
compatible Terraform, Bicep, azd, or other IaC conventions. Do not migrate them
implicitly.

For requests to implement Azure resource creation, updates or deployment
with iac-composer explicitly, or without an explicit implementation method:
- Use the installed `iac-composer` skill. Without skill support, use
  `iac_composer_get_context`, or read
  [.agents/skills/iac-composer/SKILL.md](.agents/skills/iac-composer/SKILL.md).
  Use one route, not duplicate workflow loads.
- Accept resource requirements from any planner or the user. Do not require
  a particular planning skill, generator or JSON contract, and do not repeat
  resource selection when it is already complete.
- Author `@azure/provisioning-*` Stack/resource definitions and relationships
  in TypeScript. Use iac-composer composition/helpers to validate, provision and
  deploy; Bicep is generated output, not the default handwritten source.
- Establish approved project-local dependencies and resolve references inside
  the workspace. A missing package or failed lookup does not prove a resource
  is unsupported. Prefer core `Resource` for a missing service provisioning library/type.
- Verify real resource entrypoints and `deployment.provision` steps. A single
  `deployment.deploy` wrapper around a raw Bicep/CLI lifecycle is not equivalent.
- Resolve architecture gaps separately using an appropriate planning workflow,
  then resume implementation. Advice-only and plan-only requests must not
  create files. Cloud execution requires explicit authorization.

This preference does not apply to non-Azure work, resource inventory, cost-only
questions, or unrelated production diagnostics.
